<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ukrainian SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1991 CE · capital Kharkiv</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet Ukraine, the Soviet Union's second-largest republic, which suffered the Holodomor and the Second World War and became independent Ukraine in 1991.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After two earlier attempts, Bolshevik forces proclaimed a Ukrainian Socialist Soviet Republic in January 1919, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> as capital. Despite defeats by the Whites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, the Soviet regime was established by 1920, and in 1922 the republic became a founding member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The 1920s brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainisation">Ukrainisation</a> in schools and culture, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>'s forced collectivisation caused the famine of 1932 and 1933, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a>, which killed millions. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in 1934. The republic gained western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1939, was devastated by German occupation, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1945 and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1954. Leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petro-shelest">Petro Shelest</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-shcherbytsky">Volodymyr Shcherbytsky</a> ran it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernobyl-disaster">Chernobyl disaster</a> in 1986 and the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Gorbachev</a> fed a national movement. On 24 August 1991 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verkhovna-rada">Verkhovna Rada</a> declared independence, confirmed by referendum on 1 December, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-kravchuk">Leonid Kravchuk</a> became the first president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ukrainian-ssr.jpg" alt="Ukrainian SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 and others. All authors are listed in the &quot;File versions&quot; section below. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Ukrainian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1949%E2%80%931991).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kharkiv</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Petro Shelest, Volodymyr Shcherbytsky, Leonid Kravchuk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>State atheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic, Soviet Ukraine</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1955&amp;focus=ukrainian-ssr" aria-label="Ukrainian SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5377 425 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ukrainian SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5697 681L5697 681M5697 681L5830 694M5697 681L5820 533M5697 681L5745 719"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M5830 694h0M5820 533h0M5745 719h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1955&amp;focus=ukrainian-ssr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
